course: biology
lesson: 44
title: The axolotl, the tardigrade and the mantis shrimp
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Three small animals with superpowers that are real but often told wrong: the axolotl regrows lost legs, the tardigrade survives drying out for years, and the mantis shrimp punches harder than almost anything its size.
explain: The axolotl is a salamander that never grows up: it keeps its baby gills, the feathery frills on its head, and stays in water all its life, smiling a smile that is only the shape of its mouth.
explain: Squeeze a clump of wet moss from a wall into a dish and under a microscope you may see water bears, tiny eight-legged tardigrades plodding about like chubby bears in slow motion.
explain: Games and memes call tardigrades indestructible and say mantis shrimps see a rainbow we cannot imagine; the truth is cleverer, because the tardigrade survives by shutting down and the mantis shrimp sees colours fast rather than finely.
fact: The frills on an axolotl's head are gills: it is a salamander that keeps its young features, gills and a tail fin, for its whole life and stays in water, which scientists call neoteny.
fact: An axolotl can regrow a whole lost leg with bones, muscles and nerves, and parts of its tail, spinal cord, heart and brain, without leaving a scar; doctors study it in the hope of helping people heal better one day.
fact: An axolotl hunts by suddenly opening its wide mouth and sucking in worms, insect larvae and small fish; its smile is just the shape of its mouth, even when it is ill.
fact: Wild axolotls live only in the canals of Lake Xochimilco in Mexico City, are mostly dark brown or olive with speckles, and are critically endangered because of drained, polluted water and introduced fish.
fact: Tardigrades, or water bears, are about half a millimetre long, have 8 stumpy legs ending in claws that grip moss and grains of soil so they are not washed away, and live in moss, soil, ponds and the sea; over 1,000 kinds are known.
fact: When its home dries out, a tardigrade pulls in its legs and head and shrivels into a tun, which can survive for years and come back to life when wetted.
fact: As tuns, tardigrades have survived extreme cold, huge doses of radiation and, in 2007, 10 days outside a spacecraft in space.
fact: It is a myth that tardigrades are indestructible: active wet ones die from heat or from drying too fast, and in 2019 the ones on a crashed Moon lander probably did not survive the impact.
fact: The peacock mantis shrimp strikes with club-shaped limbs at about 80 km/h, using a spring-and-catch: muscles slowly load the spring, then the catch lets all that energy go at once, fast enough to smash snail shells and to crack aquarium glass in big ones.
fact: Mantis shrimps have 12 to 16 kinds of light sensors while we have 3 kinds of colour sensors, yet a 2014 study found they are worse than us at telling close colours apart.
fact: Some mantis shrimps are smashers with clubs for hard shells, and others are spearers that stab soft fish from their burrows in the sand.
fact: Big mantis shrimps are called «thumb splitters» by divers, and a mantis shrimp is neither a shrimp nor a mantis but its own group, the stomatopods.
exercise: What are the feathery frills on an axolotl's head? | Gills for breathing in water | Ears for hearing under the water | Decorations to attract a mate | Fins for steering as it swims
exercise: What makes an axolotl different from most salamanders? | It keeps its baby gills and stays in water all its life | It lays its eggs on dry land, far from any pond | It can fly short distances | It has no legs at all, like a snake
exercise: What can an axolotl regrow after losing it? | A whole leg with bones, muscles and nerves | Only its toenails and a little bit of skin | Nothing at all | Only the skin over the wound, never any bone
exercise: Why does an axolotl always seem to be smiling? | That is just the shape of its mouth | It is always happy | It is showing its teeth | It is copying the faces of people
exercise: Where do wild axolotls live? | Only in the canals of Lake Xochimilco in Mexico City | In almost every pond and ditch across the whole of Europe | In rivers all over Africa | In the sea around Japan
exercise: What colour are most wild axolotls? | Dark brown or olive with speckles | Bright pink with a white belly and pink frills | Pure white | Blue with yellow stripes
exercise: How big is a tardigrade? | About half a millimetre long | About the size of a house cat | About the size of a grain of rice, 5 mm | Too small to see even under a microscope
exercise: Where can you find tardigrades near your home? | In damp moss on a wall or roof | Only in the deepest parts of the sea | Only inside hot volcanoes, near lava | Only in outer space, far from Earth
exercise: How many legs does a water bear have? | 8 | 6 | 4 | 10
exercise: What is a tun? | A dried-up tardigrade, curled up and almost shut down | A tardigrade egg, laid in moss and left to hatch in spring | The shell a tardigrade lives in | A baby tardigrade
exercise: What did tardigrades survive in 2007? | 10 days outside a spacecraft in space | A trip right into the middle of the Sun | A whole year in a pot of boiling water | Being dropped into the lava of a volcano
exercise: Why are tardigrades not really indestructible? | Active wet ones die from heat or from drying too fast | They are killed at once by any single drop of clean water | They live for only one hour | They cannot survive the cold at all
exercise: How fast does a peacock mantis shrimp's club strike? | About 80 km/h, one of the fastest animal movements | About 8 km/h, the same speed as a person walking | As fast as a bullet from a gun, over 1,000 km/h | As slow as a snail
exercise: How does a mantis shrimp's punch get so fast? | Its limbs store energy like a spring and a catch lets it go | It runs at the shell first, as fast as it can, and crashes | It uses electricity like an eel | It throws stones at the shell
exercise: What did a 2014 study find about mantis shrimp colour vision? | Despite many sensors, they tell close colours apart worse than we do | They see millions more colours than us and every single one is brighter | They are completely colour-blind | They see only in black and white
exercise: What does a spearer mantis shrimp do from its burrow? | Stabs soft fish passing by | Smashes crab shells with a club | Builds a sticky web to trap fish | Sucks in sand to find worms
exercise: Why does an aquarium keeper wear thick gloves near a big mantis shrimp? | Its strike can badly cut or bruise a finger | Its skin is poisonous to touch | It gives electric shocks | It sprays ink that burns skin
test: «tardigrades are literally indestructible, you could nuke one» What is right? | Only dried tuns are very tough, and even they can die | Yes, nothing can ever kill a tardigrade, not even the Sun itself | Yes, they live forever | No, they die in a single day of sunshine
test: In a game, an axolotl lives happily on land and breathes air like a lizard. What is wrong? | Axolotls keep their gills and live in water all their lives | Axolotls live only in trees | Nothing, axolotls always leave the water and grow lungs as adults | Axolotls breathe fire, not air
test: «my axolotl's smiling so he's fine, innit» What is the best answer? | Its face always looks like a smile, so watch how it behaves | Yes, a smile proves it is healthy, happy pets always smile like that | Yes, sick axolotls frown | No, smiling means it is about to bite
test: A cartoon mantis shrimp sees a magic rainbow we cannot imagine. What does science say? | It has many colour sensors but tells close colours apart worse | It sees in black and white only | It sees exactly what we see, with the same three kinds of colour sensor | It sees with its claws
test: «there's loads of axolotls in pet shops so they're not endangered» What is right? | Pets are common, but wild ones in Xochimilco are critically endangered | Right, they are the commonest animal in Mexico | Right, wild ones are everywhere | Wrong, there are no axolotls in pet shops
test: What would happen if a wet, active tardigrade were dried out very fast in a hot oven? | It would probably die, because it needs time to become a tun | It would turn into a tun at once and be perfectly fine afterwards | It would grow bigger | It would lay eggs and fly away
test: «mate stuck his finger in a reef hole to see what's in there» What is the best advice? | Never do that: a mantis shrimp or other animal could strike hard | Fine, reef holes are always empty, nothing ever lives in them, mate | Fine, mantis shrimps are too slow to hit | Only do it with your thumb
test: «mantis shrimp = a shrimp that's also a praying mantis, obvs» What is right? | It is neither: it belongs to its own group, the stomatopods | Yes, it's half shrimp and half insect | It is a kind of crab | It is a kind of lobster from the reef
test: Why could an axolotl be a help to doctors one day? | Studying how it regrows legs may help people heal better | Its smile makes sick patients happy and helps them get well faster | Its gills can be used for breathing machines | It can replace lost fingers by itself
test: Why do claws help a tiny water bear more than speed? | They grip moss and soil grains so it is not washed away | They let it dig right down to the centre of the Earth to hide | They help it fight off whales | They let it run faster than ants
test: Why does a spring-and-catch hit harder than just pushing with a muscle? | Energy is stored slowly and then let go all at once | Springs are made of a much stronger kind of muscle tissue | The catch adds a lot of extra energy from nowhere at all | It does not, pushing with plain muscle is always much stronger
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the axolotl? | It is a salamander which retains its larval gills and remains wholly aquatic | It is a fish which has learned to walk upon the bed of the lake | It is a lizard which inhabits the deserts of Mexico | It is a salamander which loses its gills and lives upon land
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a tardigrade may survive desiccation for a period of years. | It contracts into a tun, in which its bodily processes almost cease | It stores within its body a reserve of water sufficient for many years | It is constructed of a material which no force upon Earth can destroy | It burrows down to such great depths that water there is never absent
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the vision of the mantis shrimp. | Despite its many light sensors, it distinguishes close colours less well than humans | It perceives a far greater number of colours than any other animal | It is entirely without the capacity for colour vision | It possesses precisely the same three types of colour sensor as humans, and no more
exam: Ascertain the sole location in which axolotls are found in the wild. | The canals of Lake Xochimilco in Mexico City | The rivers and flooded forests of the Amazon basin | The lakes of northern Canada | The coral reefs of Australia
